# Dravidian Nationalism!
Tamil title: திராவிட தேசீயம்!

Address delivered by Arignar Anna at a large public meeting in the Kothawal Chavadi area of Madras on 4 May 1961

In this district frequented by the great mercantile community, a DMK meeting has been organised and an election-fund contribution generously made. Ordinarily, when we come to Kothawal Chavadi, we pay money, weigh the goods offered and take them away. Today, the traders must weigh and accept the goods we offer: this speech.

How shall we judge?

Suppose a worker leaves a shop after many years and then says, “That shop's scales are false; its forty-*palam* weight is short.” We would ask, “Were you not the one weighing goods there all this time? Did you not know then? Why say it now?” If he answers, “It was that way then, but this way now,” how shall we value him?

That is a question of ethics. A buyer must inspect quality and measure while purchasing; a merchant's duty is to weigh correctly.

Neither an evening clearance stall nor perishable goods

Some now reject the Dravida Nadu ideal out of personal resentment. But this ideal is not a desperate sale, an evening stall in China Bazaar shouting, “Gone tomorrow; unavailable after dawn.” Nor is it produce that rots in a week.

Until your doubts are resolved, do not join the movement. Once resolved and joined, do not doubt again. Stay outside until uncertainty is wholly gone.

If a buyer takes goods home and returns saying, “My wife says the quantity is short,” a sensible shopkeeper says, “Send her to buy next time; you need not come.”

We are not fools

Examine the Dravida Nadu policy as much as necessary before accepting it. If one meeting clarifies one matter but not another, attend another. I will wait until your doubts clear.

But if you enter saying you understand and later reject the policy, it resembles buying provisions at Kothawal Chavadi, carrying them to Royapuram, cooking and eating them, then wrapping the leftovers and returning them as “bad goods.” No merchant accepts that. This merchant, the DMK, is no fool.

Examine calmly. The idea will not disappear after 1962, nor will the eye of reason close. We conduct the movement with room for scrutiny.

A story and its meaning

A physician, astrologer and musician travelled and stayed in a ruined rest house, planning to cook. The physician went for vegetables, the astrologer for rice, and the musician lit the stove.

At the shop the physician rejected brinjal as heating and okra as liable to cause a cold in winter. He analysed every vegetable until nothing suitable remained but trays and baskets.

The astrologer saw a black cat cross his path, declared it ominous and sat for an hour. Then he calculated that his zodiac sign was unfavourable for three more hours; if he went, rice would be unavailable, fail to cook, or harm his body. He remained seated.

The musician heard boiling water beat “thala-thala” and began keeping time. When his rhythm failed to match, he declared the false beat unfit for an audience and smashed the pot with the ladle.

How can victory come?

If doubt obstructs the ideal as it obstructed those three, success cannot come. Yet the very man who dispelled doubt has now acquired it.

Educated and uneducated people, Congress members and Communists all doubted our ideal. Younger brother Sampath removed their doubts. Unfortunately, he himself now suffers them, like a doctor who cures cholera or leprosy in others and contracts the disease.

The DMK appointed Sampath chief “exorcist” to drive out the spirit of doubt. In an exorcism, the priest makes the supposed spirit declare who it is and what it wants. Sampath similarly shook doubters and told Kamaraj, “Your doubt comes from ignorance of geography; I shall buy you a globe.” Having expelled many spirits, he is now possessed by one.

Anyone with doubts may tell me privately or ask at a public meeting. Use any route, but obtain clarity.

What is nationalism?

Because “nationalism” is now invoked constantly, let me explain. We speak of beauty, but no grammar has defined the one true form of beauty. Nor can bravery be fixed in a single definition.

Congress says Indian nationalism. We say Dravidian nationalism. Some say only Tamil nationalism exists. People in the North say Indian nationalism; those outside India may say Asian nationalism; beyond Asia some reject nations for internationalism. Return from outer space and one may proclaim a single nationalism of the cosmos. No final definition exists.

I use simple examples not because I have never seen large books, but because the country is not ready for explanations built from them.

Ask everyone who can sing to stand on one side and everyone who cannot on the other. Among the singers will be tall and short people, Hindus, Muslims, Christians, Saivites and Vaishnavites. The division rests solely on singing. One might call them the singing and non-singing “nations.”

Then ask tall people to stand apart from short people. The earlier groups dissolve, and tall singers join tall non-singers, irrespective of religion. The criterion creates the grouping.

Ask people with money to stand on one side and those without on the other. Those with money may rush to the side without, fearing danger to their wealth, and everyone will appear in one group.

People who live together for generations and centuries with common thoughts and characteristics come to be treated as a national movement.

After the British arrived, they were rulers and Indians ruled subjects. That division became more important than Hindu, Muslim or Buddhist. Once the British left, the significant grouping changed again: oppressors on one side, oppressed on the other.

The oppressors were northern and the oppressed southern. The North extracted and the South was extracted from; the North commanded while the South was beaten and diminished. Northern government grew fat from exploitation while the South suffered after being stripped.

A flower shop may contain fruit before an image, and a fruit shop flowers before its deity. It is pointless to ask why a flower appears in a fruit shop. Likewise, the existence of some people within Dravida Nadu who deny it does not disprove the category. A fruit shop may contain rotten fruit and remains a fruit shop. If some reject Dravida Nadu, it remains Dravida Nadu.

Those who believe the North causes no suffering may stand with it; we do not prevent them. Those convinced northern rule degrades us are called Dravidians, and their land Dravida. Repeated conviction takes root and becomes a nationalism.

Sandal paste in a bowl will not leap onto one's chest when called. One must take and apply it to release the fragrance. Likewise, how can Dravida Nadu be achieved without effort?

Kamaraj may ask why educated people reject it. The issue is not educated versus uneducated, but whether one recognises truth.

Calling the teacher's own statement an illusion

Government rules require the flag to be raised with Rabindranath Tagore's anthem containing “Dravida, Utkala, Banga.” After singing it for years, people ask where Dravida is. Many do not even know the anthem is Bengali rather than Hindi.

Tagore did not write ignorantly. He named Sindh, regions of the Ganges and Yamuna, his Bengal and Utkal. He did not list Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Andhra and Karnataka separately; he joined them as Dravida. Why?

Manonmaniam Sundaram Pillai likewise sang of Kannada, Telugu and Malayalam in relation to Tamil. Tagore belonged to a poetic tradition and understood what he wrote. People recently celebrated Tagore as their teacher; can they praise him one day and call his Dravida an illusion the next?

Sculpture also says “Dravidian culture”

If you have no time to visit Tagore's grave, spend ten rupees on the Sunday Government bus to Mamallapuram. Official explanations label the sculptures “Dravidian culture” and “Dravidian architecture.” Towers in Andhra and halls in Karnataka are similarly classified.

If that is too far, spend eight annas to reach the Triplicane seafront. Opposite Presidency College stands the statue of U. V. Swaminatha Iyer. Do not fear Periyar's anger; he bears none toward dead Iyers! Beneath the statue the inscription calls him “Dravida Vidya Bhushana” or a comparable Dravidian honorific; the Tamil OCR reads “திராவிடக் கலாநிதி.”

Or enter the University and ask A. Lakshmanaswami what the collective department for Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam and Kannada is called. The English answer in Image 10 is destroyed by Tamil-only OCR, but the argument says it bears a Dravidian name.

Differences exist: see the evidence

At a Tamil Isai conference five or six months earlier, Ariyakudi Ramanuja Iyengar said South Indian music is Carnatic music and Carnatic music is also Tamil Nadu's music. He contrasted its modes with northern music.

Difference appears not only in music but medicine: Siddha in the South, Ayurveda in the North. Wind from the North is called *vadai*, while wind from the South is *thendral*.

Ancient Tamils who fasted unto death faced north; the practice was called *vadakkiruthal*. Even today elders tell people not to sleep with the head toward the North, though they cannot explain why. Read the old literature and seek the explanation.

Jawaharlal Nehru, praised as India's uncrowned king, wrote to Indira from prison that the region south of the Vindhyas was Dravida and the North Aryavarta. The speech attributes to him the view that despite mixture, ninety per cent of southern people continued Dravidian culture. Congress readers may consult the book in their reading rooms; I claim no authority greater than his.

A minister's occasional use of Dravida does not arise from conversion or from my eloquence. If eloquence alone were needed, there are A. Ramaswami Mudaliar, educationist A. Lakshmanaswami, Sir C. P. Ramaswami Iyer and Rajagopalachari. Above all, my leader Periyar E. V. Ramasamy confronts opposition and succeeds. I claim no greater ability than these people.

We asked those returning from Delhi; this is what they said

Finance Minister C. Subramaniam recently returned from a National Development Council meeting in Delhi. The name itself suggests nationalism still requires development; if fully present, why develop it? Chief Ministers go asking for projects, and the Council decides after debate.

We asked what he brought from Delhi. A visit to Kothawal Chavadi at least yields bananas. Would he show only mosquito bites, or the torn remnants of rejected schemes?

He replied, “I argued in Delhi not only for Tamil Nadu but for Dravida; I think Annadurai will be satisfied.” He said he asked Delhi to allot southern Five-Year Plan schemes as a total so southern States could divide them among themselves.

Another time, answering a question about the proposed advanced engineering institute at Guindy, he said it would serve not only Tamil Nadu but Dravida and again suggested this might satisfy Annadurai.

[The canonical source ends abruptly here at the bottom of Image 12. No further scan or conclusion is present, so no ending is invented.]

---NOTES---
The first-page scan identifies this as an address at Kothawal Chavadi, Madras, dated 4 May 1961. The OCR reads “4-5-67”; that error remains unchanged in the preserved Tamil source.

The canonical item contains twelve images and ends mid-speech after Finance Minister C. Subramaniam's Guindy engineering-institute statement. Translation covers every available paragraph and explicitly marks the truncation.

“தேசீயம்” is rendered as nationalism because the speech analyses the criterion by which populations form national identities, rather than merely referring to a geographical nation.

Official English labels in Images 9–10 are damaged by Tamil-only OCR. Their surrounding Tamil meaning is translated without reconstructing exact wording.

Historical, linguistic, ethnographic and cultural claims involving Tagore, Nehru, ancient Tamil custom and North-South difference are translated as political rhetoric and require independent verification before citation.
